# ID: DET-07
# Name: Everything one login session did (auid)
# Purpose: auid is the original login user and survives su and sudo, so it attributes root actions to the person who logged in.
# Data: index=soc_lab sourcetype=linux_audit (auditd)
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1078.003 Valid Accounts; supports T1136.001, T1098, T1003.008 attribution
# Reference implementation: detections/reference_detections.py::session_activity
# Expected on the synthetic datasets: Five records for auid=1002: sudo, useradd (two records), usermod and the read of /etc/shadow, all with uid 0 or 1000 although the login was svc-backup.
# False positives: None: this is an investigation query. Replace 1002 with the auid you are following.
# Tuning: Find the auid first: id svc-backup, or the auid field of the suspicious USER_CMD.
# Status: NOT TESTED in Splunk. The detection logic is tested in Python; the SPL syntax and field extractions need validation in a real Splunk.
# Lines that start with "#" are documentation. Paste only the lines below the blank line into the Splunk search bar.

index=soc_lab sourcetype=linux_audit
| rex "^type=(?<audit_type>\w+)"
| rex "\bauid=(?<auid>\d+)"
| rex "\buid=(?<uid>\d+)"
| rex "exe=\"(?<exe>[^\"]+)\""
| where auid="1002"
| table _time audit_type uid exe
| sort 0 _time
